  "parentCard": {
    "title": "The rhyming book, and they land every last word",
    "minutes": 15,
    "includes": "read-aloud",
    "steps": [
      "Pick a rhyming picture book, one with a strong bounce, and sit somewhere comfortable with them tucked in beside you. Tell them their job before you start: you are going to read, and they are going to catch the ends.",
      "Read it through once, properly, at full speed, with all the bounce you have got. No stopping and no teaching. They just get to enjoy it.",
      "Read it again, and this time stop just before every rhyme word and wait. Wait longer than feels comfortable. If they do not land it, say the rhyming word from earlier in the line again and wait once more, and if it still does not come, say it with them and carry on happily.",
      "Now be wrong on purpose. Read a line and finish it with a word that does not rhyme at all, keeping a completely straight face, and let them catch you. Do it two or three times, never more, because the game stops being funny the moment it becomes the whole book.",
      "Finish by making up one new line about somebody in this house and leaving the last word off for them. Nonsense words are allowed and the sillier the better, and the silliest one of the night gets crowned out loud."
    ],
    "notice": [
      "Do they land the rhyme from the sound of the line, or from remembering how the story goes?",
      "When they offer a made up word, do they seem to know it is a made up word, or do they think it is real?",
      "Do they start rhyming on their own afterwards, at dinner or in the bath, without anybody asking?"
    ]
  }
